<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayurveda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as āyurveda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ayurveda is an alternative medicine system with historical roots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, its name meaning knowledge of life and longevity. Its terms and concepts appear in the Buddhist Canon from the middle of the first millennium BCE, and its main classical texts, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sushruta-samhita">Sushruta Samhita</a>, date in their present form to the first centuries CE. They describe three doshas, vāta, pitta and kapha, whose balance means health, divide medicine into eight components, and teach surgery including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhinoplasty">rhinoplasty</a>. Its theory and practice are pseudoscientific, and many of its preparations contain toxic levels of lead, mercury and arsenic; it is heavily practised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</p></div>
